# Bench tuning hooks — the levers that live in code rather than in ESP-IDF's own Kconfig.
#
# The MECHANISM is defined here, once, so both targets share the symbol names. The VALUES
# belong to the rung ladders in host/rungs/<chip>/, exactly like every ESP-IDF lever: a
# rung sets CONFIG_BENCH_* and apply_rung.py writes it into sdkconfig.rung. Defaults keep
# the measurement core's behaviour unless stated otherwise in the help text.
menu "hdemg bench: tuning hooks"

    config BENCH_UDP_DGRAM_MAX
        int "Largest UDP datagram the sink sends, in bytes (0 = one per pool buffer)"
        range 0 65000
        default 1472
        help
            The UDP sink packs WHOLE frames into datagrams of at most this many bytes.
            1472 is the largest UDP payload that fits one 1500-byte Ethernet/Wi-Fi MTU,
            so no datagram is IP-fragmented (5 frames of 270 B = 1350 B).

            0 restores the original behaviour: one datagram per pool buffer (~8 KB),
            which lwIP splits into ~6 IP fragments. Losing any one fragment then loses
            all ~30 frames in it, and each datagram takes ~6 Wi-Fi TX buffers. Kept
            selectable so that cost can be measured as a rung.

    config BENCH_PIPE_NUM_BUFS
        int "Pipe pool: number of buffers"
        range 2 32
        default 6
        help
            Buffers between the source and the sink. Pool size is how long the source
            can keep generating while the radio stalls (6 x 8 KB is ~10 ms at
            40 Mbit/s). The pool is static internal RAM; a size that does not fit fails
            the build rather than the run.

    config BENCH_PIPE_BUF_BYTES
        int "Pipe pool: bytes per buffer"
        range 1024 16384
        default 8192

    config BENCH_WIFI_PIN_CHANNEL
        int "Stay on the access point on this channel (0 = any)"
        range 0 177
        default 0
        help
            For a network that serves one name from several access points. The board
            rejoins until it is on this channel, then locks onto that access point's
            BSSID. A rig setting: put it in sdkconfig.local, not in a rung. A channel on
            the other band from the build's band pin is ignored.

    choice BENCH_WIFI_BW
        prompt "Station channel width"
        default BENCH_WIFI_BW_DEFAULT
        help
            The width the station asks for. The link runs at the narrower of this and
            what the access point offers, so a 40 MHz rung only helps if the AP is set
            to 40 MHz (or 20/40 auto) on that band. The device reports both in its
            phy_rate string ("... bw<AP> sta<station>").

        config BENCH_WIFI_BW_DEFAULT
            bool "Driver default (no call)"
        config BENCH_WIFI_BW_20
            bool "20 MHz"
        config BENCH_WIFI_BW_40
            bool "40 MHz"
            help
                On a chip with 802.11ax (the C5) 40 MHz is only possible with 802.11n:
                802.11ax and 802.11ac are 20 MHz-only there. Selecting 40 MHz on such
                a chip therefore also restricts the station to b/g/n (2.4 GHz) and
                a/n (5 GHz). That trade (HE20 vs HT40) is the lever being measured.
    endchoice

endmenu
